That some children are born with more or less hair may depend on whether they were born at term, before term, or a little later.
It is very likely that you will see your hair fall out after giving birth. This is due to telogen effluvium,the process by which hair is lost three to four months after a stressful event.
Once the new hair appears, you may notice that tIt has a different tone to the original color, usually lighter. The texture is also likely to be different, as newborn hair is often very fine and fragile.
Your baby has less hair than your friend's baby, or no hair... every baby is different.
use a wipe to gently cleanse your scalp and make sure you keep an eye out for new hair to appear, because it will. Most babies will comb their hair before their first birthday.
Now, if your baby is still hairless as his second birthday approaches, talk to your doctor about possible causes of baby baldness. You can start to suspect if the baby is older than 6 months and is still losing a lot of hair.
Baby care, whether they are "bald" or with a lot of hair
use the detergent softer and more natural you can find for bedding and clothing, and stick to the basics when it comes to bathing the baby. Choose soft products, without perfumes or dyes because they irritate the skin.
Is that cradle cap?
Wash your baby's head frequently with a damp washcloth, then use a soft brush to get rid of scales.
Be sure to use only natural, fragrance-free cleansers to help reduce the risk of baby eczema.
If your baby's hair still falling after 6 monthsCheck with your doctor to see that they are not other problems such as nutritional deficiencies, autoimmune diseases or fungi.
