1).Cooling time is proportional to square of wall thickness. Long cooling time for solid will defeat the economy of mass production.
Therefore we have basic rule for plastic part design; as far as possible wall thickness should be uniform or constant through out the part. This wall thickness is called nominal wall thickness.
3).What are the considerations for deciding wall thickness?
It must also be thin enough to cool faster, resulting lower part weight and higher productivity.
A plastic part with varying wall thickness will experience differing cooling rates and different shrinkage. In such case achieving close tolerance becomes very difficult and many times impossible.
Usually we need to connect two shells together. To form an enclosed room between them to place the internal components ( PCB assembly or mechanism).
Ribs are placed along the direction of maximum stress and deflection on non-appearance surfaces of the part. Mould filling, shrinkage and ejection should also influence rib placement decisions.
Rib thickness should be between 0.5 to 0.6 times nominal wall thickness to avoid sink mark.
Rib should have 0.5 to 1.5-degree draft angle to facilitate ejection.
Distance between two ribs should be 2 to 3 times (or more) nominal wall thickness.
Linking on side may results in thick section of plastic, which is not desirable as it can cause sink mark and increase cooling time. This problem can be solved by linking boss through a rib to the side wall as shown in the sketch.
