The frame comes in two colors and three sizes to fit riders from 5-foot-3 to 6 feet tall. The Vibe 2.0 does not come stock with fenders, lights, or integrated locks, but there are various mounting bosses on the frame for aftermarket accessories.
Not wanting to venture too far afield during the pandemic, I instead made many trips down the hill to my local post office, community market, and café, and took a few cruisy rides up and down the canyon. I hauled packages to the post office both on the rack and in a backpack, and rode the 3 miles uphill home with cargo purchased at our town’s small store.
I also rode the Vibe 2.0 on wet roads several times, and while the wide tires offer plenty of traction, the lack of fenders left me covered in road grime. The chain guard helped keep grease off of my pant leg, but is noisy due to chain slap when riding bumpy terrain.
Overall, the Vibe 2.0 is a fun and comfortable ebike that is suitable for anyone wanting to bop around town, cruise bike paths, or ride to work occasionally. The Vibe is billed as a commuter bike, but the lack of integrated lights and stock fenders make it less than ideal for serious commuting.
The iZip Vibe 2.0 retails for $2,000, and can be purchased on iZip’s website or at an authorized iZip dealer. Delivery options include ship-to-home (the bike comes 85 percent assembled, with all of the necessary tools and instructions) or pickup at a local bike shop (which will assemble the bike for free).
