This article is not about how evil social media is.
The point of this article is to point out some ways that social media can be a pitfall for the Christian life and to encourage you to examine your heart when it comes to your social media consumption and participation.
This is nothing we haven’t heard before, but it’s worth repeating.
Consider for a moment what you could be doing instead of checking all those sites.
Certainly, at one time or another, we are all guilty of spending hours on Facebook in a day and never opening up the Word of God….
And I feel privileged to stand here and serve as part of this amazing effort towards unity in God’s church.
Watch as he walks through some of the chapters and gives a sneak peak at the content.
“Trip’s written a book that I think every young person needs to read.
Trip’s new book, Rise, is out now!
You can pre-order the book and find out more at Risebook.tv One of the main things I like about Trip Lee and his book, Rise, is the interplay of reverence and relevance.
The Temptation of Jesus
In Luke 4:1-2, we read about the temptation of Jesus and how He responded.
I wanted to look at Jesus’ temptation, reflect on His obedience, and think about what we can learn from this scene.
Weakness is Not an Excuse to Give In to Temptation
I pray it’s an encouragement to you
James 1:13, “Let no one say when he is tempted, “I am being tempted by God”; for God cannot be tempted by evil, and He Himself does not tempt anyone.”
That’s exactly what I was coming on here to say.
The Spirit of God led Jesus to be tempted for a specific reason.
Trip isn’t saying that God tempts us.
Why else would he send the thing most important to him, his very Son, to die on a cross for something he didn’t do?
Abraham wasn’t tempted but he was tested
Check out the video for Sweet Victory from Trip’s latest album, Rise
This is Trip’s talk from the ERLC Summit on the Gospel and Racial Reconciliation.
This evening, I’ve been asked to speak about millennials and racial reconciliation.
In Trip’s new book, Rise, he tried to write about things that are relevant to this generation.
“Trip’s written a book that I think every young person needs to read.
Here’s a sermon I preached a couple months ago on Matthew 12:36.
His passion for Jesus and this generation comes through loud and clear on every page.
That’s what your leaders asked me to talk about, but what they didn’t know is that this was already something that’s been on my heart and that I’ve been thinking about.
So why has identity been on my heart?
Because if you don’t know who you are, you don’t know how you’re supposed to live.
This man wakes up on a boat and doesn’t know who he is.
He gets in a fight and realizes he’s trained in combat.
For the rest of the movie he’s on a journey to discover who he is.
But if he doesn’t know it, he won’t be able to live as he really is.
How we perceive our identity has so much to do with how we carry out our lives.
If we don’t answer these questions we could waste our lives, living as someone else.
So if that’s who they are, that’s what they’ll pour their lives into.
And there are young ladies who go from relationship to relationship, who become obsessed with their appearance.
And when that popularity fades, they’re crushed.
If you’re confused about who you are, you’ll be confused about what you should be doing with your life—and that can have tragic results.
Student or athlete or rapper or pastor?
We can’t let our friends define us.
We have to let God define us because He’s the authority on the matter.
I could have talked about a million things Scripture says about us, but I had to divide it into three.
That’s the main point of this session: all of us are beautifully made, but broken.
Turn there if you have your Bibles.
This whole Psalm is about how God knows everything and is everywhere at the same time.
He knows what he’s going to say before he says it.
He’s in heaven, He’s on the earth, He’s everywhere.
Then starting in verse 14 he praises God as the Creator.
I praise you because I am fearfully and wonderfully made; your works are wonderful, I know that full well.
When I was woven together in the depths of the earth, your eyes saw my unformed body.
That’s the starting point of our identity, and we should live in light of that reality.
Now I’m assuming most people here tonight understand that God is our Creator.
Contrary to what many say, we’re not here by coincidence or chance.
We were intentionally created by God.
It makes a difference in our lives and shapes our identities.
Now try to answer that question, what are you here for?
That means family doesn’t really mean anything, our morals are pointless, my hard times have no purpose.
And fortunately, it’s untrue.
Where you come from says a lot about who you are.
The person who made it.
We want to hear from our Creator, who made us.
What does it mean to be fearfully made?
It wasn’t haphazard or random.
All of it was on purpose.
There’s this bowl my mom still has on the shelf in our house.
It’s not that I wasn’t capable of doing better.
I just wanted to hurry up and finish so I could talk with my friends.
He did it with great care.
Think of a skilled sculptor, carefully chiseling away until his masterpiece is beautiful.
We are wonderful creatures.
He goes on to say, “your works are wonderful, I know that full well.” He praises God for His wonderful works.
He’s done amazing things.
In fact, we’re the most wonderful of all of them.
Human beings are incredible.
A few years ago I had a tear in my lung and couldn’t breathe.
A few days later I was fine.
We are broken, but still amazing.
How do we know we’re the most wonderful part of creation?
No animal, or plant, or piece of land, or planet is created in God’s image.
God created everything to show off His glory, and we’re the part of creation that gets to show it off most clearly, because we’re the part that’s made like him.
We’re like statues of God in creation.
We represent him and we get to show a lesser picture of him.
You need this.
The guys or girls may not think you’re attractive, but you know what you’re fearfully and wonderfully made.
Your jokes may be corny, but you are fearfully and wonderfully made.
And nothing can take that away!
You are fearfully and wonderfully made.
What God thinks matters a lot more than what you or others may think.
He has a song called “I Am a God.” So many people think this highly of themselves.
God alone is creator.
Nobody is worthless.
There’s no such thing as a nobody.
But we pay people to care for our son while we’re gone.
God didn’t just create everything and then let it go.
“My frame was not hidden from you when I was made in the secret place.
He’s saying that even when he was in the womb God saw him.
They don’t know much about him yet.
And we could already hear a heartbeat.
If God forgot about you, you would know.
None of us are invisible.
This should be a comfort to us.
You don’t have to fight for significance—you’re already significant.
Even if you never got to shake their hand, it would be enough that they just knew who you were.
Shouldn’t that be enough?
My dad used to say all kinds of things to me that drove me crazy.
But another thing he used to say is, “Boy, I know you better than you know yourself.” It would make me so mad.
But my dad knew.
Our little human brains can’t comprehend everything.
God controls everything and He knows EVERYTHING.
He knows what you’re thinking right now.
Only God could know so much about all seven billion people on earth.
Because God is the One who made us and He can tell us.
This is praiseworthy!
God has never found something out.
He’s never made a decision before getting all the facts.
Doesn’t this make you want to trust Him?
But as I’ve already mentioned, some of us think too highly of ourselves.
We forget that we’re also broken.
We’re not only wonderfully made; we’re also broken.
We were flawless and perfectly connected to God.
And while we used to be whole, now we’re broken.
It’s in pieces.
I just got this iPhone not too long ago.
But before I got this new one my iPhone was terrible.
I didn’t want to spend the money on a new one yet though.
I thought it was amazing.
I would realize it may be messed up, but it’s still amazing.
I can still take good pictures on it.
I can still download stuff on it.
Apple did an amazing job on that phone.
In a similar way, God did an amazing job on us, but we are broken.
We get hurt by others.
Do you ever think about that reality that you’re broken?
We get fooled because we don’t seem any different than anyone else around us.
I want to look at two primary ways that we are broken.
When we’re young we live under the impression that we’re doing just fine.
Maybe small signs, but we ignore them.
It started to make noise, but she just kept driving it.
Everything was taken from him.
Here’s what he says:
He comes out like a flower and withers; he flees like a shadow and continues not.” (Job 14:1-2)
We don’t have an infinite number of years to live.
We may come out beautiful like a flower, but we will wither.
And eventually we will die.
It comes to an end.
There’s no amount of science and technology that can solve the problem of death.
Death is a reality that Google cannot solve.
We’re the most amazing part of creation, but ever since the Fall we are also broken.
We shouldn’t assume that our young bodies that work so well now are what we’ll have forever.
So the first way we’re broken is physically.
“None is righteous, no, not one; no one understands; no one seeks for God.
Their throat is an open grave; they use their tongues to deceive.
Their mouth is full of curses and bitterness.
And here, here’s basically telling the Jews, yes even you.
“Surely there is not a righteous man on earth who does good and never sins.” (Ecclesiastes 7:20)
So of course compared to other people I’m a good person.
I’m not a terrorist.
But what we should really be interested in is God’s perspective.
It’s all true.
We say we know we’re broken, but we’re unwilling to admit we’re not right in a situation.
Maybe we just argue, or maybe we blame them instead.
You’re flawed, which means you can be wrong.
We have a heart condition and the heart is where everything comes from.
Sin is the thing that keeps us from God.
We may think scary people or bad situations are our worst enemy.
Other things can harm us for a season, but because of our sin we may be punished forever.
Some of us think we’ve never committed a serious crime.
He’s much higher than the authority of a police officer, or the president.
And even if we haven’t murdered, the fact that we’ve lied is still a heinous crime against God.
We reflect God’s image, but not like we should.
Instead we show an ugly distorted picture.
A desire to provide turns to greed.
It’s all sin, and God hates it.
That’s the bad news.
We think only good people go to Heaven, and it’s true in a sense.
So we need someone else to give us their goodness.
This means we can’t put our hope in our physical or spiritual selves.
We’re not good enough.
If we were just spiritually broken or just physically broken, maybe it wouldn’t seem like that big of a deal.
Or just spiritually, we’ll never have to face God because we’ll never die.
Do you know you’re needy?
There’s a Savior who meets our greatest need.
Jesus is the only one who can restore what is broken.
He’s our only hope.
He rose from the grave.
Jesus paid it all.
So to recap: who are you?
He looks down and still recognizes us as the crown of His creation.
If you haven’t really acknowledged that you are broken, that you’ve offended God, that you need a Savior.
If you’re not trusting in Christ, then you’re not a child of God.
How does it change your life knowing that God sees all?
Can’t wait for the concluding part.
