He was trying to listen quietly, but his facial expression responded before his mouth had a chance.
He gave me a confused look and calmly asked, “Why are you taking life so seriously, young man?
Youth is the time when you mess up a lot, and that’s okay.
I was stunned.
I reflected on that experience for a long time, but instead of dampening my fire, I think it turned up the heat.
I had to get up and live.
Waking up is my least favorite part of every day.
But when it’s seven in the morning, staying in bed is like winning the lottery.
Because there’s pretty much no chance I’ll get up after the thirteenth alarm.
Sometimes I think I could convince myself of anything in those first drowsy minutes of each morning.
I could tell myself all kinds of lies, like, “Yeah, you should go to class, but will it really matter?” or “I know you’ve been late every day for the last three weeks, but what’s another day?” or “Maybe my friend will take the test for me.
Somehow an extra five minutes or an extra hour would improve my life.
Can you imagine what the world would be like if nobody got up until they felt like it?
Nobody would have enough time to do his job well; by the time everyone woke up, half the day would be gone.
Unfortunately, many of us have adopted 7:00 a.m. logic as a way of life.
We don’t feel like doing something in the moment, so we decide to put it off until later.
But procrastination doesn’t actually solve anything, so it’s a bad idea to delay daily tasks until the last minute.
Our culture doesn’t usually expect much from people until they’re old.
They say, “You’ll bear the burdens of responsibility for the rest of your life, so enjoy your youth while you can!” People seem to expect us to take all of life lightly until we reach that magical, arbitrary age of responsibility.
He wasn’t much younger than me, probably in his early twenties.
I asked him all the usual small-talk questions: Where are you from?
Will you spit in my food?
He told me that he had only lived in Phoenix for a few months.
At this point I began asking myself how I’d describe him to a police sketch artist, just in case he was a fugitive of some sort.
I don’t want to stay in one place and take on a bunch of responsibility.
It’s my time to just explore, not be bogged down with a bunch of commitments.
I was sad but not surprised by his response.
Should we hold off on all convictions, commitments, and seriousness until later?
I heard a song the other day that captured this perspective perfectly.
I know taking on Taylor Swift hasn’t gone well for people in the past (do I need to remind you about Kanye?).
I think she has successfully captured the feelings of her listeners and the spirit of the age.
What does it mean, as she says in the song, to “feel twenty-two”?
Unfortunately, it suggests that this happiness and freedom is found in confused, sometimes miserable wanderings.
Swift didn’t make this perspective up; we’ve been sold this logic over and over again.
Whether or not this 7:00 a.m. logic is trustworthy depends on who you are and what you were created for.
If you’re nothing more than another person looking out for yourself, then that young waiter’s logic may seem pretty sound.
But what if you were created for something more?
