My heart is heavy.
Yet, two thousand years ago Jesus of Nazareth was murdered by his own malicious assassins, and we remember that day as “Good” Friday.
I learned about hedonism, casual sex, materialism, irresponsibility, laziness, drugs, and getting respect – all as pieces to the puzzle that is the good life.
How can I get respect?
How can I run the world?
And when young people turn on the radio, though there is a lot hip-hop that sounds good, so much of it is filled with lies about what the good life really is.
The reason I zoomed in on hip-hop is because it’s my culture and reality.
And my self-centered, glory-hungry heart ate them up.
I was confronted with a new picture of the world and a new picture of life.
I didn’t want to live life in light of lies anymore.
If we continue to believe lies about how we should live, the consequences are major.
We’ll waste our lives chasing after stuff that doesn’t matter.
We won’t experience true joy and satisfaction.
We won’t live the good life.
So I want to get at what the best kind of life is biblically.
So I want to look at three things the good life is.
The Good Life is Living By Faith In a Good God
Living by faith in a good God.
WE ALL LIVE BY FAITH
Because you believe it will make you better at your sport (unless you were me growing up playing basketball and still being really bad).
Some of you put the address in Google Maps to get here today.
Because you believed that your phone would give you the right directions.
I could go on and on.
I talk about this in the book.
My wife is consistently amazed at how dumb I am with directions.
Similarly, all of us are fallen.
The devil feeds us lies, the world distributes them, and our hearts eat them up.
And the best one to tell us about how to best live this life is God Himself.
Our problem is we believe the lies of the enemy over the truth of God.
God created Adam and Eve.
We believe our flesh when it tells us to look at that website.
We believe those lies instead of God’s truth.
The good life begins when we turn from our sins, we decided to leave all of our disobedience behind, and we put our faith in Jesus.
But our faith doesn’t end there.
FAITH IS AN EVERYDAY FIGHT
And often we think when we sin, it’s just because we had this moment of weakness.
And it’s because we don’t believe God when he says He put authority in place for our good.
And it’s because we don’t believe God when He says that sex is for married couples, and that’s the context where it’s rightly enjoyed.
So how do we fight for that faith?
Our spiritual life begins with faith in the Word and it continues with the same.
So we fight those lies with truth.
We run out of gas everyday.
That fuel is God’s Word.
Not to say if we don’t read the Word one day we’ll be atheists when we wake up.
I feel the unbelief crowding out faith in various areas of my life.
We need God’s people.
Do you just attend church or do you spend good time with others?
We need to be in close relationships.
When I was dating my wife, a huge thing was people in my life holding me accountable to our boundaries.
You will be able to live the way we were created to live.
What does it look like when we live by faith in a good God?
The Good Life Is Built Around Jesus
All of us have heard people talk about death from time to time.
But as a young Christian, I read a verse about death in the Bible that was like nothing I had ever heard before.
Paul didn’t just say death was certain, he took it a step further.
Death means the conclusion of life and separation from family members.
Unlike our other trials, death is, for the person who dies, literally “the end of the world” – the end of this one anyway.
I can’t make money when I die?
It just didn’t fit with my old views of the good life.
In Philippians 1 Paul explains why he seems to be OK with either staying alive or dying at the hands of his persecutors.
How could my self-centered, status-obsessed worldview survive next to that truth?
I’ve noticed that the most successful players in any sport, the champions, all have something in common.
And their obsession, this deep desire to win, trumps all of their other desires in the game.
Surely they think about that stuff, but at the end of the day, that’s not what they play for.
So if they have an opportunity that would benefit them, but not the team, they pass.
In life, that thing Paul is obsessed with.
That passion that supersedes all of his other passions is Christ.
Paul says in Philippians 1, that he could live or die; and he’d be fine because Jesus would be honored.
Life is Christ.
Jesus is the merciful Savior who stood in our place and offers us new life.
Jesus must be the motivation for all of our decisions.
It’s ALL about Jesus.
To live is Christ.
So this changes the way that we see everything.
We were created to live in a way that shows Him to be who He is.
One time, I was in Dallas and I went to one of my favorite fried chicken spots, William’s Chicken.
Drive through service was bad, but then I get to the front.
And he says, “Oh, my bad I forgot about you bro.” What do you mean you forgot?
That’s what you do at work.
He got distracted.
We forget what we’ve been put here to do.
Laboring for Christ doesn’t mean we all need to be preachers.
So it doesn’t mean we should all go change what we do or aspire to do, though maybe it will.
It’ll look different in different lives, but we can do it to His glory.
In their view the good life available to us in Christ is a life free from any trials now.
That even though I suck at basketball, through Christ I can be an NBA all-star?
Because I have Christ.
God doesn’t demand status, he demands faithfulness.
I’m challenged when I read Paul’s words, especially considering Paul’s situation.
But the lens he sees it through changes everything.
Sometimes we throw a fit like a child in the midst of trials, because we think every circumstance is about us.
We throw grown up temper tantrums because we think our circumstances are ultimately about us being comfortable.
They’re about Jesus.
Instead of leading to heartbreak they lead to more joy.
Friendship is more enjoyable when built around Him.
You can be freed up to enjoy money, when you don’t worship it.
Those who build their life around futile things set themselves up for destruction.
It’s a gift from God and it can’t be taken away.
How do we get it?
The Good Life Is A Gift From a Loving God
The lie we’ve been told is that God wants to keep us from the good life.
But one of the biggest differences is how we get there.
And even for some of those who work the hardest to achieve it, it remains out of reach.
It’s a free gift that’s available to all.
