The Perfect Anger of God
My 3 points were simple:
I can imagine someone asking the question, “How could something this terrible make me stronger?” Even the most positive people can be broken down by the brutality of our fallen world.
“What doesn’t kill me makes me stronger” can be true, but only if there’s something beyond this life.
You’d have to ignore the truth to believe that.
All of us will face trials, but we can’t finally be defeated by them.
So how exactly can these devastating trials actually make us stronger?
When I feel like everything in my life is going well, my heart immediately retreats into self-dependence.
I start to pray less because I subconsciously assume I don’t lack anything.
In those times, I’m forgetting that God is the giver of good gifts, and I still need Him to sustain me.
But when trials come our way, they show us our own weakness.
2 Corinthians 12 is a comfort to me in times of weakness.
How could Paul be content and even glad about His trials?
Self-dependence is weakness, and dependence on Christ is strength.
We’ll never be able to endure devastating trials until we realize that our comfort and good health isn’t what’s most important.
But God’s primary will for our life is that we would be like Jesus.
Hebrews 12 says, “ disciplines us for our good, that we may share his holiness.” Training in God’s gym may hurt sometimes, but He will make us stronger.
Our humility is more important than our happiness.
Our hearts are weakened by sin, and we often find ourselves longing for more of what this world has to offer.
Our citizenship is elsewhere.
But then I start to miss my wife, and then I realize I don’t have the clothes I meant to bring, and then I realize room service is about half as good as my wife’s cooking.
Unmet desires make me long to be at home.
There will be no unmet desires in Heaven, because our Lord will satisfy all of our longings.
So the next time you have a friend going through a tough time, remind them that even the most tragic circumstances can make us stronger.
And when you’re going through a difficult season, let it draw you closer to Christ.
The believer’s hope is beyond the grave, and in the next life we’ll be like and with Him.
Going through a super tough time and His faithfulness to comfort his children never fails to amaze me.
This was a little surprising to read.
But, again, it’s through God’s glory that we can manage through the tough times and realize that there is something greater than this world.
