Does becoming a Christian mean the end of my fun?
There’s a certain picture of Christianity that’s often painted.
Some think screaming TV preacher, others think white guy in a suit or old black grandmother in a big church hat.
But very few people think Christianity and think: hey, that would be a fun thing to do!
One rapper in a song says, “If you scared, then go to church.” Apparently Christian churches are for timid, scared punks.
First of all, some of y’all aren’t cool in the first place, but that’s another story.
He was talking about his experiences with churches.
He thought that meant he had to make gospel records now.
As if becoming a Christian is the last thing you’d do if you wanted to enjoy yourself!
Here’s how fun is defined in the dictionary.
There’s no question that becoming a Christian means your understanding and experience of enjoyment, amusement, and pleasure will change.
There’s no doubt that becoming a Christian changes the way you see life.
Has everyone in our culture gotten it right?
I think one of the reasons people talk about Christianity this way is because of the way Christians are portrayed in the media.
They’re usually judgmental and corny.
The second reason is because some Christians and those who call themselves Christians give off that vibe.
Some Christians really don’t enjoy anything and they blame it on God, as if it’s HIs fault.
I have two answers to the question for tonight.
My first answer is: No
No – Becoming a Christian Does Not Mean the End of Your Fun
“Does becoming a Christian mean the end of enjoyment, amusement, and pleasure in my life?” It says something about God to ask that question.
To answer yes says something untrue about Him.
Of course he wants you to enjoy the life that He created.
Did he want you to just suffer through it and hate it?
Life is different, because it’s not just some form of entertainment, but there are similarities.
He created life. He made us in His image and He wants us to live that life to the full.
When you read the Bible that’s just not the picture you’re going to get.
Instead you’ll find God wanting us to enjoy our lives.
People think God wants to keep us from good things, but that’s a lie.
Do not be deceived, my beloved brothers.
They paid for everything so what do you mean?
God created friendship and marriage.
In John 10, Jesus contrasts Himself with false teachers.
Jesus calls them thieves.
They think God wants to steal.
That God wants to kill us and drag us down.
He wants us to live our lives to the full.
In Psalm 16, David is talking to God and here’s what he says.
So coming closer to God doesn’t lead to boring sadness, it leads to joy.
I could go on for days with passages like this.
It’s actually the road to true enjoyment, amusement, and pleasure.
Last week, my wife and I went to see Gravity in IMAX 3D.
But what if instead of those glasses we brought our sleep masks from home?
You don’t see anything but darkness.
We’d miss the whole movie.
It was like we were in the movie.
The glasses brought that experience to life.
He’s not trying to block our joy, and leave us in the dark.
He wants us to enjoy life as it was meant to be enjoyed.
Our problem is we’re under the impression that fun means doing whatever we feel like in any given moment.
Who wants to be a slave to everything they feel like doing?
My son wants to have fun and he thinks sticking his finger in the outlet is the best way to do that.
And if my son is going to have fun instead of dying he has to trust me.
But as an adult, I know that he doesn’t walk well and he’ll tumble down.
We would all acknowledge that we’re not all-knowing.
And because we’re sinners, we value the wrong things.
That brings me to my second answer to this question.
My second answer is: Sometimes.
Sometimes – Following Jesus Will Mean the End of Some of Our Fun
Becoming a Christian does mean you will have to deny yourself.
Drunkenness seems fun but leads to stupid decisions.
It’s the deepest way you can give yourself away to someone else.
As we’ve already acknowledged, God created all of life and He knows how it’s supposed to be enjoyed.
At His right hand are pleasures forevermore.
God doesn’t keep us from the good life; we keep ourselves from the good life when we disobey Him.
And he said to all, “If anyone would come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross daily, and follow me.” (Luke 9:23)
Denying yourself means saying no to yourself.
You probably wouldn’t put that on a hallmark card.
If we were always good and wise asking us to deny ourselves would be strange.
If we’re misled and messed up, asking us to deny ourselves is helpful.
There are things that you enjoy that are bad for you.
Jesus is saying if you want to follow me, you have to stop following yourself.
If you realize you’ve been going the wrong way, and you ask someone to help you, you can’t keep going your way.
So Jesus is saying, let go.
You’ve given yourself to me.
I’m the Leader.
If my wife said, if you want to be my husband you have to deny yourself and follow me.
If my son said that… I’d really be surprised since he can’t talk.
Even if my pastor said, deny yourself and do whatever I say no matter what it is, I’d go to another church.
Because I don’t want to give the reins of my life completely over to another sinful person.
They have their own issues.
He’s holy, He’s my Creator, and He died so sinners could be reconciled to God.
He knows what my life should look like.
Being a Christian means that you sign up to say no to yourself sometimes.
Because we’re wrong regularly and we need His help.
In it, he talks about how everything is meaningless.
So you could take this, as here we go.
God doesn’t want me to enjoy it.
Listen to what he says later:
For he will not much remember the days of his life because God keeps him occupied with joy in his heart.
I thought he said it won’t satisfy you.
Money doesn’t satisfy you, it just makes you want more money.
You can enjoy something, without living under the false assumption that it will satisfy your soul.
Our problem is that we want to worship the gift instead of the Giver, Creation instead of Creator.
Christians are not trading fun for boredom, or joy for sorrow.
They’re trading deadly joys, for life giving joys.
We live by faith in our Creator.
Whether we realize it or not, all of us are people of faith.
Christians, Muslims, Hindus, Buddhists, Agnostics, Atheists—all of us live by faith, we live in light of what we believe in any given moment.
If you believed your phone would trick you, which I think mine does sometimes, you wouldn’t have used it.
But we all live by faith.
He tells us how to enjoy His creation rightly.
Any time we treat anything other than God as an end in itself we’re not enjoying it correctly.
But maybe you think, I don’t want that.
Being amused, enjoying yourself is not all life is about.
If you build your life on the foundation of fun, it’ll come tumbling down when life is hard.
Why wait to start living?
“We are half hearted creatures fooling about with drink and sex and ambition when infinite joy is offered us, like an ignorant child who wants to go on making mud pies in a slum because he cannot imagine what is meant by the offer of a holiday at the sea.
Then I became a Christian.
Unlike our other trials, death is, for the person who dies, literally “the end of the world”– the end of this one anyway.
In Philippians 1 Paul explains why he seems to be ok with either staying alive or dying at the hands of his persecutors.
Therefore to enjoy things rightly, we have to enjoy them in a way that testifies to that truth.
When we treat anything like an end itself it’s wrong.
Should we just have sex with as many women as possible?
Sex Is best enjoyed in a marriage covenant.
There’s humility, forgiveness, grace, love.
Power is best enjoyed when it’s not worshipped.
Wine in moderation is better than drunkenness.
But think about how they relate to the meaning of all life: Christ.
We are broken, and Christ came to put us back together.
And we deserve to be judged for those offenses.
Jesus didn’t have a problem with enjoying the wrong things, or enjoying good things in a sinful way.
He rose from the grave and offers us eternal life.
We will not live our best life now.
