Millennials and Racial Reconciliation — TRIP LEE - OFFICIAL SITE
Every day there are concerts like this all over the country.
It happens all the time with concerts, or sporting events, etc.
Those people don’t really have to be unified.
They just have to tolerate each other for a couple of hours.
That kind of unity isn’t lasting.
These people aren’t really serving one another.
The kind of unity we’re after is more lasting and more substantial— more loving.
The kind of unity we’re after can only be produced by the Gospel of Jesus.
Consider these words from John 11:
Our end goal is not just getting black and white people in the same room.
Our goal is to proclaim the peace and unity that Jesus has already accomplished and calling people into THAT.
And we want that for every generation.
Transition: The millennial generation is of course those born in the early 80s to the 2000s.
But what about that deeper, more substantial, and more difficult kind of unity?
If you are a millennial like myself, then I want you to consider ways we can continue to fight for reconciliation.
That goes for pastors, church members, parents, friends, etc.
It’s not easy to call people both to be reconciled and to encourage them to call others to the same.
Of course things are very different now in this country than they once were.
So, many of us assume that we’re in such a completely different time, which makes race relations no longer a relevant conversation.
Racism only was—it’s probably like five generations ago…Racism is for—I wouldn’t say generations.
This is what many millennials think….“We’re now in a post-racial society.
Sadly, some recent studies have shown that millennials do give more lip service to equality than past generations, but we’re still basically just as prejudice as our parents were.
Of course, recent events even like racist chants among the Oklahoma fraternity show that racism is alive and well in our generation.
Racial prejudice is often more subtle, but that doesn’t mean it’s less sinful.
And that can make it tricky to fight.
We’ve even seen social media challenge and mobilize people in light of the recent racial issues in the U.S.
Some people call this slack-tivism:to retweet activists, preachers’ quotes, and blog posts on these matters, and to stop there.
And having social media friendships with other ethnicities and cultures is good, but it’s definitely not enough.
Some millennials don’t think that God’s Word is sufficient in our day.
And because of that, our own confidence in the Bible is shrinking.
And how is a message supposed to make a real difference any ay?
To them, all we need is Christian action —as if proclaiming the Gospel isn’t an action.
If we are church leaders or members of churches or even parents, how do we address this in the lives of my generation?
I think each of these addresses all three of the above problems.
Solution #1: Preach the Gospel of Reconciliation
I’ve just moved to Atlanta to help plant a new church, but before that I was on staff at a church in D.C. and a member there for four years.
There were more and more black members, Chinese members, older members, and younger members.
It wasn’t perfect diversity, but it was beautiful.
Why does diversity happen?
But I, when I am lifted up from the earth, will draw all men to myself
Jesus said when he’s lifted up, meaning when He would die on the cross, he would draw all men to himself.
He doesn’t discriminate here.
More than just Israel, but all other nations as well.
This is the heart of our conversation here.
The root of racism and racial tension and racial separation is sin.
We have to proclaim that message.
Revelation 5 says Jesus, “…purchased for God people from every tribe, language, people, and nation.” This has been God’s plan all along, and we get to tell it!
It’s our primary weapon.
Gospel unity goes much deeper than many of us realize.
That’s much deeper than the other unity we’re used to.
We millennials need the same Gospel everyone else does.
So, let’s continue trusting it and proclaiming it, even if our culture doesn’t want to hear it.
But let’s not forget about what the Judge of all has already handed down.
Ok, so what other solutions are there beyond just proclamation?
“A new command I give you: Love one another.
By this all men will know that you are my disciples, if you love one another.”
This love he talks about here is a holy affection for somebody and an unselfish commitment to their good.
We should be committed to each other’s good.
Every interaction we have with one another should be characterized by love.
He became poor for us, endured a sinful world for us, and laid his life down for us.
And that’s how people will know that we are his disciples.
The rest of the New Testament puts a little but of flesh on what this love looks like.
Don’t ignore part of who I am
We have to understand each other.
How can I be compassionate towards you, if I don’t know the pressures that weigh on you?
A couple of years ago, I was having a conversation with a close friend of mine who happens to be white.We were having a mushy brotherly love type of moment.
I don’t even see you as black.” Now I appreciate what he was trying to communicate to me.
But on the other side, I think that’s an unhelpful way to view others.
I don’t even notice that you’re single.” Well, he is single.
And so I encouraged my friend— not to think mainly about my blackness every time he talks to me— but also not to pretend like it’s not there.
And I can only speak for myself and my black experience.
There’s an extra burden that I, and many black people, have to carry.
An awareness of this changes the way you love someone.
Many occasions, it’s been assumed that I’m a criminal whenI couldn’t be further from that.
Many occasions it’s been assumed that I’m inferior to my white peers or that I’m not as good.
Not to mention people referencing my loved ones in hurtful ways.
There are patterns of this in my life and in the lives of many.
But why does this matter?
Because of that command to love one another.
We have unique joys, burdens, and experiences, and you can not love others well if you dismiss or ignore their unique experiences.
She had only been in the states for a short period of time, and as she talked about her experience with the sermons, she explained how difficult it had been for her to follow.
It totally changed the way I viewed the experience of sisters like her in our church, and it helped me to think more carefully about how to love them.
So, we need to go back to Jesus to work it in us.
Solution #3: Don’t Assume It’s Easy
When someone comes to our church, and they see diversity—a kind of diversity they don’t see in the world—it gives them a glimpse of the glory of the Gospel.
Every church—even if people are of the same ethnicity, age, background— has issues because we’re sinners.
We all bring our own backgrounds, experiences, presuppositions, biases, and baggage along with us.
Scripture is not unaware of this conflict.
So, since it will be hard, here are a few random tips for how to work through those difficulties:
Their differences aren’t primary.
I can think of a time when I was back in Philly, when a professor would always talk to me differently.
He could’ve been aware of my differences without feeling like he had to talk to me in a different way.
Some stereotypes are funny and harmless, but others are offensive.
Intentionally build relationships with people who don’t look like you.
After church, purposely talk with people who are different.
Listen carefully and sympathize with people’s unique experiences.
Diversity is hard, especially when we start talking about race/ethnicity.
If someone seeking to understand you offends you, be gracious to and patient with them.
When seeking to understand someone, don’t be afraid to ask questions or say something wrong.
Don’t give up when it’s hard.
That’s a question we should all be asking ourselves.
We should all question the way we think about people who are different than us, including different culturally and ethnically.
What comes to mind when you see them in the store?
This should be one of many conversations.
This is meant to start other discussions.
And if we never talk about it, we won’t be aware and work towards it.
If we don’t talk about it, we run into the danger of continuing to assume that this problem has already been dealt with in the past.
You could wake up one day to fire!
Unity is a big deal, so we should pray that God would grow our passion for it.
We’re commanded to realize the reality that we’re one in Christ.
Look at Ephesians 4.
Look at how it all ends in Revelation.
But, as we’ve said many times already, only the Gospel can produce this kind of sacrificial love and unity.
We want to labor to put this Gospel on display for the world to see.
One He uses to make His Gospel shine brighter.
