There are three simple options below with the BRAG logo.
Whenever I post pictures of my family on social media, the responses are always fun.
This is irrelevant to some, but shocking or even disappointing to others.
The decision to marry someone from a different ethnic background wasn’t a tough one for me.
Though if I did, the fact that my wife has never seen an episode of “Martin” would be in the con category.
I was convinced that she was the woman for me to marry, even though she wasn’t black.
Early Retirement One of the responses I wasn’t surprise by was this one: “Wait, I thought you retired?” This is the question that follows me around like a shadow.
Here’s what I did know: there would be a huge priority shift that would drastically change how I related to music.
I knew I wanted to help pastor God’s church.
I’ve done admin work, preaching and teaching, youth ministry, marital counseling, etc.
Health issues have made it challenging at times, but it’s been a rewarding season for my family and me.
After that first year of full-time pastoral ministry, I decided to slowly begin working on a new record.
But instead of a five or six month burst of non-stop writing and recording, this album was slow and steady since music wasn’t my full time job.
I decided to work with one producer this time, instead of accepting track submissions from a bunch of different guys.
Every trip I made to Atlanta was crucial because it was so hard to get away from family and church duties.
I know some people are still confused about what to expect from me in the future.
– I still want to be a pastor.
– I still want to travel to rap and speak.
And I’m trying to find out how or if I can do all of those things in a way that honors God, while still being faithful to my family and God’s church.
It could be, but I hope not.
A lot has changed since 2012, but one thing remains the same: Jesus is Lord, and I want to honor Him with my music.
