5 Pitfalls of Social Media
Let me start by giving a disclaimer.
We are reminded in Ephesians 5:16-17 that the days are evil and we should make the best use of the time given to us.
While waiting at an appointment, we could keep a list of people to pray for on our phone and spend those extra minutes in prayer for our family and friends.
Since every moment is a gift from the Lord, it’s worth asking yourself, “Is there something more worthwhile that I could be spending my time on?” Most of the time, the answer is probably yes.
Social media gives a platform to your every thought
We complain, we anonymously bash people, we disrespect people in authority, and we say whatever we think about celebrities, as if they weren’t real people.
Don’t be fooled by the privacy of your room right now—there will be nothing private when we stand before the living God and give an account for our words.
When we spend extended time pouring over what others are doing and saying, it draws our attention to all the things we don’t have or are missing out on.
Why didn’t I think of that quote?
Their new house is way bigger than ours!
Hebrews exhorts us, “Keep your life free from love of money, and be content with what you have, for He has said, ‘I will never leave you nor forsake you.’” We can ultimately be content with our lives as believers because we are given all that we need in Christ.
Social media can quickly become our own podium for self-promotion.
Are we looking for sympathy or praise?
Do you constantly check how many “likes” or “retweets” your post got?
This can be a particularly hard pitfall for anyone who has a platform that extends beyond their friends and family.
We must remember that retweets, comments, and likes don’t automatically give legitimacy to what we post.
As I Samuel 2:3 cautions, “Do not keep talking so proudly or let your mouth speak such arrogance, for the Lord is a God who knows and by Him deeds are weighed.”
Social media can distort our view of relationships
It is easy to keep up with the ones you love (maybe too easy?).
It is easy to keep up with a lot of people you love—and a lot of people you don’t even know (maybe too many?).
We can quickly train ourselves to be consumers of information instead of lovers of people.
Of course Jesus is the perfect example of what it means to be a true friend.
Greater love has no one than this, that he lay down his life for his friends.” Friendship is more than the sum of status updates and pictures.
It’s bearing each other’s burdens.
I will openly admit that I waste hours and hours of my time and days on social media and all the points brought forward, I can directly relate to.
