It goes something like this…
You’re married?
Me: I’m twenty three.
Why in the world would you do that?
In 2009, I got married at the barely legal and barely respectable age of twenty one years old.
What’s the rush?”, and my personal favorite, “You should have waited and enjoyed your life!”
Was I crazy to get married at such a “young” age?
As an eighteen year-old freshman in Bible college, I wasn’t actively looking for a wife, but God saw fit to introduce me to a young woman after his heart.
She was aware that she was a sinner in need of God’s grace, and she had a humble, teachable heart.
I began to talk to my inner circle about her.
I could see myself walking with her and loving her for the rest of my life.
I asked myself whether or not I was mature and responsible enough.
After lots of prayer and conversation, my mentors and the pastors in my church told me they thought I was ready to pursue her.
But I wouldn’t have pursued her if I thought I wasn’t prepared to spiritually lead her, provide for her, and to be the head of a family.
But when I looked in the Scriptures I didn’t find stuff like, “Marriage equals death!
Instead I found commands like, “Flee youthful lust”, and I read passages like Proverbs 18:22 that says, “He who finds a wife finds a good thing and obtains favor from the LORD.”
Marriage is a beautiful gift from God!
I found a wife, and that is a good thing.
Instead I would say enjoy God’s good gifts and steward them for His glory.
It would have gone bad if I would have tried to get married before I was ready.
My goal is not to say that the younger you get married, the more holy you are.
My answer?
It’s one of the best decisions I ever made.
I don’t want to make it seem like I’m some super rare anomaly.
They too saw marriage as a gift and they’ve seen the fruit of that gift.
Until then I want to obey the wisdom of the Proverbs and “rejoice in the wife of my youth.”
The Bible doesn’t say anything about dating around or interacting with others to find that one.
He didn’t date around or talk to Leah to so if she was better so he would know whether or not Rachel was really what he wanted or not.
Not only from my own relationships, but from my parents’ and sisters’ relationships.
I did find a Godly Man, so it can work out the way that you say, BUT I wholeheartedly believe that if I would have waited and guarded my heart from those other relationships that were not right for me, I would have been in a much better and less broken position, to recieve my husband from a Godly woman’s perspective rather than a broken woman’s perspective with baggage to work through.
The Bible can say whatever it wants, but you’re experiences are all you have to go off of.
In the end, what you believe is a choice, but what you know is imposed upon by reality.
The only thing that has been proven is that you have made a conscience decision not to live your life for God through his word, the bible.
The fact of the matter is, one will not have the complete understanding of this blog if God is not put first in one’s life.
I’ve made a conscious decision to find God for myself and not by what someone has taught me.
